                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>Tm2</td>
                    <td>1,2</td>
                    <td>What is the behavior when the time synchronization signal/messages are lost?</td>
                    <td>Two SNTP Time Servers are supported. If primary server is not available, secondary server is taken into use. If both primary and secondary servers are not available, IED will go to unsynchronized state. For more behavior see Tm9.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>Tm3</td>
                    <td>1,2</td>
                    <td>SNTP Request Interval</td>
                    <td>60 seconds</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>Tm4</td>
                    <td>1,2</td>
                    <td>Time tagging of events</td>
                    <td>Overall time synchronization accuracy is T1 for computed events. Time tagged process events have a hardware delay up to 3 ms.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>Tm5</td>
                    <td>1,2</td>
                    <td>When is the time quality bit "Clock not synchronized" set?</td>
                    <td>After 3min timeout when SNTP server has not responded.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>Tm6</td>
                    <td>1,2</td>
                    <td>Is the timestamp of a binary event adjusted to the scan cycle?</td>
                    <td>Y</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>Tm7</td>
                    <td>1,2</td>
                    <td>Does the device support time zone and daylight saving?</td>
                    <td>Y</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>Tm8</td>
                    <td>1,2</td>
                    <td>Which attributes of the SNTP response packet are validated?</td>
                    <td>Y Leap indicator not equal to 3? Y Mode is equal to SERVER Y Originate Timestamp is equal to value sent by the SNTP client as received by the RX/TX timestamp fields are checked for reasonableness. Y SNTP version 3 and/or 4</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>Tm9</td>
                    <td>1,2</td>
                    <td>SNTP time synchronization behavior</td>
                    <td>Start: IED will read the absolute time and will set its internal clock accordingly. Looking for an SNTP server: During this phase no time data are produced. Synchronized to an SNTP server: The accurate SNTP algorithm is acquiring the data needed to reach full accuracy. The SNTP server in use is: Meaning one of the following: - It sends a "keep-alive" message. - It is not synchronized.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>Tm10</td>
                    <td>1,2</td>
                    <td>SNTP time quality behavior</td>
                    <td>When time change occurs(e.g. one hour jump in server), time quality will go first to zero and then stabilize to 10 bits after 2 to 5 SNTP requests.</td>
                </tr>
